From: Christopher Stenger <chris.h.stenger@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] drm: h713-afbd: give userspace a well-formed size range

GStreamer's kmssink aborts every caps query against this driver:

    GStreamer-CRITICAL: gst_value_collect_int_range:
        assertion 'collect_values[0].v_int < collect_values[1].v_int' failed
    GStreamer-CRITICAL: range start is not smaller than end for `GstIntRange'

It builds its caps from drmModeRes before it has chosen a mode:

    "width", GST_TYPE_INT_RANGE, res->min_width, res->max_width

and GstIntRange requires start < end. This driver reported min == max ==
the panel size, so width and height never made it into the caps at all and
the sink was left advertising formats with no geometry.

min == max is the obvious way to say "fixed-size scanout" and it is what the
other fixed-panel tiny drivers do -- sharp-memory, repaper, pixpaper -- so
kmssink is broken for all of them, not just for this one. Patching GStreamer
is out of scope for this project by standing constraint, and the driver can
give the same guarantee in a form userspace parses.

Open the MAXIMUM rather than lowering the minimum, and the ordering matters:
lowering the minimum to 0 was tried first, on hardware. It makes the range
well-formed and the criticals go away, but it also lets a source negotiate
320x240, which this plane cannot scale -- so an un-sized pipeline stopped
failing at negotiation and started failing with -EIO at commit, which is later
and less legible. With the minimum at the panel size, anything smaller is
excluded while caps are still being negotiated, and fixation lands on the
panel size by itself.

These bounds constrain framebuffer CREATION, not scanout: an oversized
framebuffer can be allocated and simply cannot be used, because
drm_atomic_helper_check_plane_state rejects a plane that would have to scale.
4096 is the ceiling h713_afbd_read_mode() already applies to the adopted
geometry.

Measured before and after on the panel, 1000 frames of 720p HEVC decoded on
the VE and displayed through the stock pipeline
(v4l2slh265dec ! videoconvert ! kmssink): identical 24 fps, and the criticals
are gone.

Signed-off-by: Christopher Stenger <chris.h.stenger@gmail.com>
---
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/tiny/sun50i-h713-afbd.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/tiny/sun50i-h713-afbd.c
@@ -356,10 +356,39 @@
 	ret = drmm_mode_config_init(drm);
 	if (ret)
 		return ret;
+	/*
+	 * The scanout is fixed-size, and the obvious way to say so is
+	 * min == max -- what the other fixed-panel tiny drivers do
+	 * (sharp-memory, repaper, pixpaper). It is legal, and it breaks stock
+	 * userspace: GStreamer's kmssink builds caps from drmModeRes before it
+	 * has chosen a mode,
+	 *
+	 *     "width", GST_TYPE_INT_RANGE, res->min_width, res->max_width
+	 *
+	 * and GstIntRange requires start < end. With min == max every caps
+	 * query aborts with "range start is not smaller than end", width and
+	 * height never reach the caps, and the sink advertises formats with no
+	 * geometry at all.
+	 *
+	 * The minimum stays at the panel size and only the maximum opens up.
+	 * That ordering matters and the other way round was tried first:
+	 * lowering the minimum to 0 makes the range well-formed, but it also
+	 * lets a source negotiate 320x240, which this plane cannot scale, so
+	 * the failure merely moves from negotiation to a -EIO at commit. With
+	 * the minimum at the panel size, anything smaller is excluded while
+	 * the caps are still being negotiated, and range fixation lands on the
+	 * panel size by itself.
+	 *
+	 * These bounds constrain framebuffer CREATION, not scanout: an
+	 * oversized framebuffer can be allocated and simply cannot be used,
+	 * because drm_atomic_helper_check_plane_state rejects a plane that
+	 * would have to scale. 4096 is the same ceiling h713_afbd_read_mode()
+	 * already applies when it sanity-checks the adopted geometry.
+	 */
 	drm->mode_config.min_width = h->mode.hdisplay;
-	drm->mode_config.max_width = h->mode.hdisplay;
+	drm->mode_config.max_width = 4096;
 	drm->mode_config.min_height = h->mode.vdisplay;
-	drm->mode_config.max_height = h->mode.vdisplay;
+	drm->mode_config.max_height = 4096;
 	drm->mode_config.funcs = &h713_afbd_mode_config_funcs;
 
 	ret = drm_vblank_init(drm, 1);
